{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Restore the branch_orders database on node1 with the supplied primary keys, exact monetary values, and customer/order relationships.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final database contains customer IDs 10 and 20 and order IDs 101–103 with amounts 12.35, 99.90, and 0.05. The schema retains both primary keys, DECIMAL(10,2), and the customer foreign key; the orphan count is zero.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f4100149c2a047ce","cmd-fdb0008182434401"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Provide a node2 reporting account that can read customers and orders but cannot change them, while retaining administrator write access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node2 account authenticated remotely and read both tables. Its grants are limited to SELECT on customers and orders, and attempted UPDATE and DELETE operations were denied without altering data. Root retained ALL PRIVILEGES and completed transactional write probes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-97149a0a571346ba","cmd-f4100149c2a047ce","cmd-fdb0008182434401","cmd-177c56232028465d"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the supplied SQL export.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The export's final SHA-256, 452-byte size, mode 0644, and timestamp match the captured prepared baseline and initial executor observation.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-1825419419a44078","cmd-f4100149c2a047ce","cmd-fdb0008182434401"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS Stream 10 with kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64. MariaDB 10.11 was installed from the native EL10 AppStream repository and demonstrated working service and client behavior.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d1a3482198a7430e","cmd-1825419419a44078","cmd-89ef8d4478cc49c6","cmd-fdb0008182434401","cmd-177c56232028465d","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.92,"summary":"No state-changing command lacking a clear task, prerequisite, verification, restart-protocol, or cleanup role was identified. Package installation, database configuration/import, restricted firewall access, credentials, and reboot probes were causally connected to the outcome. The hygiene concern is that the reporting credential was briefly served over plaintext HTTP on port 18080; access was source-restricted to node2 and the listener, firewall rule, source credential, PID, and logs were subsequently removed. Final snapshots show healthy package state, no failed services, no abandoned transfer files/listener, and only the required node1 database listener. Thus there were no unnecessary mutations, but the temporary credential-transfer method modestly weakened security while active.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d2878acbded540c7","cmd-1e92c72721db4f3c","cmd-01282dcc62354dd1","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed on both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service/data or reporting access.","limitations":["Reporter write denial was behaviorally sampled with UPDATE and DELETE rather than every possible write operation; direct SELECT-only grants on both tables provide the broader authorization evidence.","The temporary credential transfer used plaintext HTTP, although it was restricted to node2 and fully cleaned up."]}
